> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Windows 10 1607+ / 11 x64 only.** Uses Win32 GUI, Job Object, WinEventHook, low-level input hooks, per-monitor DPI awareness v2, and a local Microsoft Edge install.
>
> You do **not** need to install anything into Edge first: this program opens a standalone window with `msedge.exe --app=<tokenUrl>`.

---

## What it does

From dsh 0.1.2 the web root is authenticated: each process prints an authentication URL with `?token=`. The browser exchanges it for a cookie and lands on the clean root. Without a valid cookie or the current process token, the root returns 401. The token can be exchanged repeatedly during that process's lifetime; it is not consumed on first use.

This repo folds that flow into a console-less exe:

1. Single-instance check: if an instance is already running, just bring its window to the front and exit.
2. Show a native splash immediately (cancellable).
3. Launch `dsh web --no-open` through pnpm's `node.exe`, stdout on a pipe.
4. Parse `dsh web: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/?token=...`.
5. Open Edge `--app=<tokenUrl>` once, maximize it, then publish a tray icon.
6. Closing the window (X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W) only **hides it to the tray**; the backend keeps running.
7. Tray menu → Quit → Job Object kills the node tree and frees the port.
8. Tray or market restart → wait for authenticated readiness, validate the HTTP exchange, then swap the app window; the tray icon remains.

---

## Close and restart

| Action | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| Left-click the title-bar X | Hide to tray, backend keeps running |
| `Alt+F4` (app window focused) | Same |
| `Ctrl+Shift+W` (app window focused) | Same |
| Left-click the tray icon / run the exe again | Bring the existing window to the front |
| Right-click tray icon → Show window | Same |
| **Right-click tray icon → Restart backend** | **Reclaim and relaunch the backend, and swap in a fresh app window (a full refresh, new page)** |
| **Run the exe again with `--restart`** | **Same as the tray item (handy for a shortcut)** |
| Restart from dshmarket | Wait for an owned listener, a complete auth log line, and a verified token exchange, then swap the window |
| Right-click tray icon → Quit and stop backend | Close window + kill process tree + remove tray icon + exit |
| Window destroyed by anything else (taskbar preview close, browser crash, …) | Reclaim the backend and exit too — no zombie processes |

None of the three "hide to tray" paths shows any notification, and none of them stops the backend.

Hotkeys are intercepted only while the **app window is focused**, so they never steal `Alt+F4` / `Ctrl+Shift+W` from other programs.

### Restart readiness

The market helper and replacement remain in the launcher's Job Object. Reclaiming the job as soon as the old process exits would kill them. A listening port is not sufficient either: DSH listens first, then finishes plugin loading and startup auditing, and only then prints its authentication URL.

The launcher completes a market restart in this order:

1. Require a new listener inside the current Job, excluding the old PID and unrelated processes.
2. Keep reading the tail of `%TEMP%\\dsh-market-restart-*.out.log`; accept only complete token lines for the same loopback port and root path.
3. Do not discard a log by modification time. On Windows, a live stdout writer may keep mtime stale. The current server, not a timestamp, determines whether a token is current.
4. Verify with a new WinHTTP session: token request → **303 and a `dsh-auth-` cookie** → root request with that cookie → **200, HTML, and the DSH boot marker**. Do not use Edge cookies, proxies, or external redirects.
5. Only then adopt the backend and swap the window. Re-arm the destroy hook before closing the old window. If the old window was hidden, hide the replacement too.

The observation budget is 60 seconds. Each HTTP operation has a one-second timeout bounded by the remaining budget. An empty job, auth timeout, or window-swap failure is reported as ERROR and reclaims the owned backend; it is not silently reported as success.

The tray item and `--restart` obtain the URL from their own stdout and run the same HTTP check and window swap. You need not close the window manually, but its handle changes and a visible window may briefly switch.

**Verification boundary:** the HTTP check proves that the current host accepts the token and newly issued cookie. It does not inspect Edge's cookie jar, React assembly, or every plugin's rendering. This launcher does not fix DSH revision 404s, hot-update black screens, or notification-plugin bugs. The market log naming convention remains an integration dependency.

The v2.3.2 fake tests omitted persistent log writers and delayed readiness output; their pass did not establish that a real market restart was fixed. The regression suite now covers those cases and distinguishes fixture tests, real DSH HTTP verification, and full UI acceptance.

