## Quick start

### 1. Prerequisites

- Windows 10 1607+ / 11 x64 with Microsoft Edge
- Global dsh **≥ 0.1.2**: `pnpm add -g @deepseek-ai/dsh`

### 2. Create shortcuts

Download the [latest release zip](https://github.com/2bitbit/dsh-desktop/releases/latest), extract it, then in PowerShell:

```powershell
# Interactive: folder picker
.\install_shortcut.ps1

# Explicit workspace
.\install_shortcut.ps1 -TargetDirectory "D:\Your\Workspace"
```

Shortcuts land on both **Desktop and Start Menu**. Clicking one is equivalent to running `dsh web --no-open` in that directory, then opening a maximized Edge app window with the process token.

The tray icon comes from the exe's embedded resource (`src/tray_icon.ico`); the shortcut icon uses the same one.

---

## Lifecycle

```
Shortcut (--cwd <dir>)
        │
        ▼
dsh_desktop.exe ── single-instance check (already running → focus it, exit)
        │
        ├─ splash
        │
        ├─ Job Object ──▶ node (dsh web --no-open)
        │                     │
        │                     └─ stdout: dsh web: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/?token=***
        │
        ├─ Edge --app=<tokenUrl>  (maximized)
        │
        ├─ tray icon published
        │
        ├─ X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W ──▶ hide to tray (backend keeps running)
        │
        ├─ tray "Restart" / --restart / dshmarket's Restart
        │        └─▶ owned listener + complete auth output + verified HTTP exchange
        │               ├─ verified ──▶ adopt the process and swap the window (tray remains)
        │               └─ timeout / empty job / swap failure ──▶ report error and reclaim
        │
        └─ tray menu "Quit" ──▶ TerminateJobObject ──▶ reclaim process tree / free port
```

While running it sleeps on `SetWinEventHook` + `MsgWaitForMultipleObjects` — **zero polling, 0% CPU**.

How the X gets intercepted: the launcher installs a `WH_MOUSE_LL` low-level mouse hook. On left-button-down inside the window's top band it asks the app window for `WM_NCHITTEST`; Chromium answers truthfully for its self-drawn title bar, returning `HTCLOSE` for the close button. Only an `HTCLOSE` hit swallows the click and hides the window — normal clicks inside the page are untouched.

---

## CLI

| Option | Description | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `--cwd <path>` / `-w <path>` | dsh working directory | `dsh_desktop.exe --cwd "D:\Projects\Demo"` |
| `--debug` / `-d` | debug log + error dialogs | `dsh_desktop.exe --debug` |
| `--restart` | Ask the running instance to restart the backend (same as the tray item) | `dsh_desktop.exe --restart` |

Two log files next to the exe (truncated on each launch, appended while running, size-capped; tokens redacted):

| File | Contents | Cap |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `dsh_desktop.log` | launcher log | 512 KB |
| `dsh_web_console.log` | `dsh web` stdout/stderr | 2 MB |

---

## Build from source

Needs one of MinGW-w64 / LLVM Clang / MSVC, plus the matching resource compiler (`windres` or `rc.exe`):

```powershell
.\scripts\build.ps1
```

The script first compiles `src/dsh_desktop.rc` (which embeds the tray icon `src/tray_icon.ico`) into a resource object, then links it together with `src/dsh_desktop.cpp`.

Output: `bin/dsh_desktop.exe`. The GCC path links with `-static -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++`, so the binary does not depend on MinGW runtime DLLs.

> The GCC path is tested on MinGW-w64 GCC 15; the Clang and MSVC paths are untested.

### Isolated regression suite

```powershell
.\scripts\test.ps1
```

Requires MinGW-w64 GCC and Node. Tests use private temporary directories, OS-assigned ports, and child processes they own. They do not open Edge or operate on the user's DSH instance. Coverage includes live log writers, stale mtime, complete lines, delayed output, stale tokens, exchange responses, Job/PID ownership, and timeout. Passing the HTTP fixture is not full market/UI acceptance.

